he dust settled, the outlaw Black Bart's gunmen were down — winged, not killed; Ada Coleman never shot to kill if winging would do — and the outlaw Black Bart stood alone, empty-handed.\n\nthe outlaw Black Bart's claim died that afternoon, in public, on the record. Copper Basin exhaled. the ranch foreman bought the drinks. Ada Coleman drank one, slow, tasting justice. It tasted like dust and sweet water."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "Ada Coleman organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the outlaw Black Bart's middlemen. the ranch foreman rode drag. a wanted poster — the deed — rode in Ada Coleman's breast pocket, the reason for it all.\n\nthe outlaw Black Bart's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the outlaw Black Bart never understood.\n\nThe dust tested them: stampede at midnight, Ada Coleman riding the point with a lantern and a prayer; the river crossing, the ranch foreman swimming the lead steer; the long dry stretch, sharing the last canteen."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "Copper Basin threw a dance. the ranch foreman danced every dance. The schoolteacher cried. The blacksmith forged a wanted poster's likeness in iron for the courthouse wall — 'so nobody forgets,' he said.\n\na wanted poster stayed in the courthouse, under glass, with the iron likeness beside it. Proof. Promise. a person's word is their bond — carved, at the ranch foreman's insistence, on the frame.\n\nBut Ada Coleman stayed through the winter, helping the ranch foreman mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of Copper Basin instead of just its defender. It suited them."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "Copper Basin is still there, in the valley, under the big sky. The water runs. The cattle graze. The deed holds. Travelers ask about the iron a wanted poster in the courthouse, and the old-timers tell it true.\n\na person's word is their bond. In Copper Basin, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nthe ranch foreman keeps a room ready, and the kettle on, and the news of Copper Basin in letters. 'Come home when the trail allows,' every letter ends. The trail always allows, eventually."}]}

